      "math_academy_id": "e-1089",
      "problem": "Given that $a=2x^3$ and $b=\\dfrac{x}{y},$ express $ab^2$ in terms of $x$ and $y.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute for $a$ and $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{b}^{2}=2{x}^{3}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}=2{x}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply and apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) & = \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{3+2}}{{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{y}^{2}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
